<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1.0/policyconfig.dtd">
<!--
  Chronicle backup engine — polkit authorization (spec §11).

  The engine (chronicled.service) runs as root and exposes its verb surface on
  the Unix socket /run/chronicle/engine.sock. A desktop user's GUI
  (org.intergenos.Chronicle) is unprivileged, so the daemon authorizes every
  NON-root peer before serving a verb (chronicle/api.py authorize_verb ->
  pkcheck against the peer's credentials). Root peers — the `chronicle` CLI
  under sudo and the pkm pre-transaction handler during a package transaction —
  are already privileged and skip the check.

  These are PURE authorization actions (no exec.path): the daemon is the
  privileged side and does the work itself after the check; there is no separate
  setuid runner to launch. Enforcement is fail-closed — an unauthorized or
  unverifiable peer is refused, never served.

  TWO ACTIONS, because two different questions are being asked.

  .read — "is my backup healthy?" Served for status, queue-status, list and
  target-scan. These return machine-level backup health and per-version
  metadata: whether a target is attached, free space, when the last capture ran,
  how many changes are queued, which versions exist and how many files each
  holds, and which devices could serve as a target. No file path and no file
  content appears in any of them; the daemon additionally strips the `scope`
  field from queued capture intents before answering a read-tier caller,
  because that one field can carry paths. yes for a local active session: the
  person sitting at the machine may read the state of their own backups without
  a password. Remote and inactive sessions still authenticate, because "sitting
  at the machine" is the whole basis for granting it.

  Reading this state was previously an administrator action, and that was a
  defect rather than caution. Requiring a password for a harmless read trains
  the user to clear administrator prompts on reflex, which is the reflex an
  attacker needs; and it produced repeated dialogs during ordinary use.

  .manage — everything that changes state (capture, restore, scrub,
  retention-apply, pin, unpin, target-adopt) AND the read verbs whose responses
  can disclose another user's data: manifest (the file list of a version), diff
  (a stored-vs-live oracle for a named path), verify (its problem strings embed
  paths) and restore-plan (per-path actions). config.user_data_paths defaults to
  ["/home"], so one shared store holds every user's home directory with no
  per-user partition; a path out of that store is somebody's business other than
  the caller's. Gating a read verb costs one password. Freeing one wrongly costs
  privacy, so anything ambiguous stays here.

  ON auth_admin_keep AND CACHING — READ THIS BEFORE RELYING ON IT. An earlier
  version of this file stated that the user "authenticates once and the
  credential is cached for the session, so a browse-and-restore GUI session does
  not re-prompt on every verb". That sentence has been removed because it was
  promising a behaviour this project has not demonstrated. The daemon authorizes
  a unix-process subject, one per connection, and the client is typically a new
  process each time; whether polkit's keep-caching then applies to a subsequent
  process in the same session was never measured here, and the observed
  behaviour was a fresh dialog per client process. auth_admin_keep is retained
  because it can only ever reduce prompting relative to auth_admin, never
  increase it — but no part of the system may assume a cached credential will be
  there. If the caching is later measured to work, say so here with the
  measurement; do not restore the claim on the strength of the flag's name.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <vendor>InterGenOS</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/InterGenJLU/intergenos</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>org.intergenos.Chronicle</icon_name>

  <action id="org.intergenos.Chronicle.read">
    <description>Read Chronicle backup status</description>
    <description xml:lang="en_US">Read Chronicle backup status</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to read Chronicle backup status</message>
    <message xml:lang="en_US">Authentication is required to read Chronicle backup status</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="org.intergenos.Chronicle.manage">
    <description>Manage Chronicle backups and restores</description>
    <description xml:lang="en_US">Manage Chronicle backups and restores</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to manage Chronicle backups and restores</message>
    <message xml:lang="en_US">Authentication is required to manage Chronicle backups and restores</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin_keep</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin_keep</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
